update public.curriculum_units as c set description = v.description from (values
('181d511a-45b1-45ff-837f-c7a8a05c0539'::uuid, 'After absorption, nutrients are transported to cells and used in different ways. In this lesson, you will explain assimilation and describe how absorbed glucose, amino acids and other nutrients contribute to energy release, growth, repair and storage in the body.'),
('20b02e68-0d02-4d38-a020-6352fafe18a5'::uuid, 'Digestion and absorption make nutrients available, but a healthy body also depends on obtaining the right balance of them. This section examines the components of a balanced diet and how nutritional needs vary with age, activity and other conditions. You will connect dietary choices with growth, energy supply and good health.'),
('35670af8-b5c9-4e77-8f9c-6d06bbe8cb2c'::uuid, 'The alimentary canal is the continuous passage through which food moves during digestion. This section follows its main organs from the mouth to the anus and explains how each part contributes to the movement, breakdown and absorption of food.'),
('3703d2b3-8f7e-4af1-889e-ccc127bed5b3'::uuid, 'Mechanical digestion physically breaks food into smaller pieces without changing its chemical composition. In this lesson, you will examine chewing, churning and peristaltic movements, and explain how they help mix food with digestive juices and increase the surface area for enzyme action.'),
('3914baf1-32c9-43f8-a58b-445c975a8b4e'::uuid, 'A balanced diet supplies the nutrients needed for normal body function and good health. In this lesson, you will identify the main dietary components, explain their roles and consider how requirements change with age, activity and physiological condition. You will relate suitable food choices to energy needs, growth and maintenance.'),
('3c1ed211-a306-48be-b745-6ea1f3ed1081'::uuid, 'Digestion makes food substances small and soluble enough to be absorbed. In this lesson, you will distinguish mechanical from chemical digestion, explain why large food molecules must be broken down and identify the main stages through which food is processed in the alimentary canal.'),
('3d9af79c-b49a-4998-b542-142cf0b067f2'::uuid, 'The alimentary canal is made up of organs that receive food, move it along and carry out digestion and absorption. In this lesson, you will identify the main organs in order and describe their roles, from the mouth and oesophagus through the stomach and intestines to the rectum and anus.'),
('4a46b91b-a863-4227-8940-6e4e956337c8'::uuid, 'Before following food through the digestive system, establish what digestion achieves and why it is necessary. This theme introduces the breakdown of large, insoluble food molecules into smaller, soluble substances that can cross the gut wall and be used by cells.'),
('4e8026bf-aa4e-4b45-9a0f-d150b02aa7f9'::uuid, 'Digestive enzymes speed up the breakdown of food molecules into absorbable products. In this lesson, you will identify important enzymes, the food substances on which they act and the products formed. You will relate where each enzyme works to the conditions found in different parts of the alimentary canal.'),
('51afaad3-abf8-4680-aada-b0e8a74ca520'::uuid, 'Chemical digestion depends on enzymes that act on particular food substances. This section examines the main digestive enzymes, the nutrients they act on and the products they form, connecting enzyme action with the stages of digestion in the alimentary canal.'),
('5d1e1911-4166-4925-9669-012ce8baf74a'::uuid, 'Digestion prepares food for absorption, but the products must still cross the wall of the alimentary canal. This theme focuses on the movement of soluble nutrients into the body''s transport systems and the adaptations that make absorption efficient.'),
('72f39022-628c-4780-add8-393dcdde4e9f'::uuid, 'Once nutrients have entered the blood or lymph, they are delivered to tissues. This theme examines what happens next: how cells and organs take up absorbed substances and use them for energy, growth, repair and storage.'),
('7742cb07-c8b5-4f1c-9a22-0a264c3b9d18'::uuid, 'Absorption is the movement of digested nutrients from the alimentary canal into the body''s transport systems. In this lesson, you will explain where most absorption takes place and describe how the villi and microvilli of the small intestine provide a large surface area and support the uptake of nutrients.'),
('7c97dfcb-ed3c-43d4-a45a-51aa695a1c97'::uuid, 'Before enzymes act on food molecules, physical processes help break food into smaller pieces and mix it with digestive juices. This section examines mechanical digestion, including chewing and muscular movements that increase the surface area available for chemical action.'),
('885ec909-aea5-4126-9d48-a5ce8f99e367'::uuid, 'The body needs a suitable supply of nutrients to support the processes discussed throughout this unit. This theme brings together the main components of a balanced diet and the reasons nutritional requirements differ between individuals.'),
('96736fc9-c239-42b0-afea-bd78f2328620'::uuid, 'After food has been broken down, the soluble products must pass into the body for use by cells. This section examines absorption, focusing on where it occurs and how the structure of the small intestine supports the movement of digested nutrients into the blood and lymph.'),
('980731b4-cd1c-4116-b2cf-d7d2f34a679c'::uuid, 'Absorbed nutrients are transported to different parts of the body, where cells use them for energy, growth and repair. This section explores assimilation and explains how absorbed substances are taken up and used by tissues according to the needs of the organism.'),
('b6e1e4a7-0838-4f55-96f1-4cec6a9e8c0d'::uuid, 'Mechanical digestion changes the size and form of food, while chemical digestion changes the molecules themselves. This section examines how digestive substances break large food molecules into smaller products that can be absorbed through the gut wall.'),
('bb5950ef-688b-4d50-ab4a-949a7a180c38'::uuid, 'Chemical digestion breaks large food molecules into smaller soluble products through reactions involving digestive enzymes and other digestive substances. In this lesson, you will compare the digestion of carbohydrates, proteins and fats and identify the smaller products that can be absorbed.'),
('f1fbb8d1-bc6e-45b8-894e-ac5fb6f5fb32'::uuid, 'Food contains large molecules that cannot be used by the body until they have been broken down into smaller, soluble substances. This section follows the process of digestion, from the entry of food into the alimentary canal to the breakdown of nutrients and their preparation for absorption. You will examine the organs and processes that make nutrients available to the body.')
) as v(id,description) where c.id=v.id and c.description is distinct from v.description;
